# Family A13: the ActiveMQ protocol (internal/activemq/protocol).
# See hack/bakeoff.sh.
PKG=./internal/activemq/protocol
BENCH=BenchmarkActiveMQProtocol
REF=amqp10
AUTO_KEY=activemqprotocol
KIND_DIM=""
KINDS_TEST=TestProtocolKindsList
# ADR-013 collects the §9 rows that need a broker and are therefore decided in
# Part B.
ADR=13
TITLE="ActiveMQ protocol: AMQP 1.0 against STOMP, on Artemis and Classic (A13)"
# This family's arms ARE the two drivers -- drivers/activemq over
# github.com/Azure/go-amqp and drivers/activemq/stomp over
# github.com/go-stomp/stomp/v3 -- so no package belonging to either of them
# could hold both, and a root-module `go test` cannot reach a nested driver
# module anyway. What lives here is the part of each
# protocol that is a DECISION rather than a client library: the
# supported-version floor, the §4.8 feature subset, the destination form, and
# the property mapping both drivers run on every publication and every
# delivery.
#
# There is no AUTO_FILE=- : this family DOES bind an Auto constant, because
# which protocol a caller gets by NOT choosing is a real default. That is the
# difference from A11 and A12, whose arms are separate MODULES a caller selects
# by which one they import, and from A10 and A29, whose kind is chosen per call
# or by a build tag. Here both arms are in one root-module package and
# `New(Auto)` has to answer with one of them, so hack/bakeoff.sh's default --
# `$pkg_rel/auto.go`, i.e. internal/activemq/protocol/auto.go -- is exactly
# where `bakeoff decide` should write the binding and no override is needed.
# TestAutoBindingMatchesADR reads AUTO_KEY back out of the ADR front matter.

#
# CRITERION is the benchmark configuration's decision column for this row, quoted.
# MATRIX is the IQMQ_BENCH_MATRIX grid the campaign measures the family at.
# DECIDED_AT names the benchmark-name segments the criterion is read at, and
# DECIDED_ON the metrics; `make bench-lint` lists the family's benchmarks at
# the declared grid and fails when a term or a metric is missing from the rows
# every kind has in common, which is the only data `bakeoff decide` reads.
CRITERION="on Artemis and Classic; feature coverage score (delay, groups, priority, TTL) -> feature completeness on Artemis, then perf -> AMQP 1.0 default; STOMP kept for Classic"
# MATRIX=default is the only grid: internal/activemq/protocol has no
# IQMQ_BENCH_MATRIX branch. The row states its own sweep inside the benchmark
# name -- two protocols, two BROKERS, the mapping's two directions and §10.2's
# own header count -- so there is no wider grid to ask for.
MATRIX=default
# The BROKER is a benchmark-name segment and not only a reported metric,
# because "on Artemis and Classic" is the first clause of the row's criterion
# and the mapping really does different work on the two products: over AMQP 1.0
# the delay goes in a different SECTION on each, and a de-duplication key is
# carried on Artemis and refused on Classic. A family measured on one broker
# would report a number for a criterion written about two.
DECIDED_AT="carry restore artemis classic headers=0 headers=4"
# The FIRST of the criterion's terms is a fact rather than a measurement, and
# so is supported-version compatibility, so both are reported as metrics on
# every row -- the scorecard then carries the whole criterion rather than the
# half of it that has a clock attached:
#
#   features    the coverage score, as the number of the §4.8 inventory the
#               protocol supports NATIVELY on that broker. It is computed from
#               Protocol.Support rather than measured, and the four conformance
#               suites are what prove the table in both directions. It is FOUR
#               numbers because the row's criterion is read on Artemis AND
#               Classic: 19 and 16 for AMQP 1.0, 15 and 13 for STOMP.
#   min-broker  supported-version compatibility, as the major version the
#               protocol needs on that broker. Also four numbers: Artemis 2.7
#               for both arms (the subscription is an FQQN), Classic 5.9 for
#               AMQP 1.0 and 5.6 for STOMP.
#
# ns/op and allocs/op are the "then perf" term, for the part of it that is
# honestly measurable with no broker: the property mapping each protocol's
# message model demands, on the hot path of every publication and every
# delivery. Read them with the caveat bench_test.go's reportProtocol states --
# the STOMP arm validates every header value as UTF-8 and every header name
# against the frame's own because its mapping is what builds the frame, and the
# AMQP 1.0 arm's typed sections need neither, so a faster STOMP row would be
# evidence that it checked less. That is why the row puts feature completeness
# FIRST. TestAllocs pins both arms at zero allocations in both directions on
# both brokers.
DECIDED_ON="features min-broker ns/op allocs/op"
# What is not measured here, and where the evidence for it lives instead.
# NB: entries are separated by ";" and nothing inside an entry may contain one,
# or the parser reads the fragment after it as a key with no text. And no
# BACKTICKS in a value: hack/bakeoff.sh SOURCES this file, so a `word` inside a
# double-quoted assignment is command substitution -- the first draft of this
# line printed "features: command not found" on every dry run and stored the
# entry with the word missing.
CRITERION_GAP="throughput against the real brokers=needs two brokers and two client libraries -- the four conformance suites run the same §11.2 suite through both drivers against apache/activemq-artemis:2.44.0-alpine and apache/activemq-classic:6.2.0 (make integration DRIVER=activemq and DRIVER=activemq-stomp) and ./conformance/matrix exercises the version floors; the feature coverage score's own truth=the features metric is computed from Protocol.Support rather than measured, and the same four suites are what prove each row in both directions; the four measured behaviours the tables rest on=a STOMP NACK returning nothing, an Artemis modified disposition ignoring x-opt-delivery-delay, a selector being ignored under an FQQN subscription and no STOMP delivery carrying an attempt count are all broker behaviours no microbenchmark can produce, and the critical scenarios of drivers/activemq and drivers/activemq/stomp are where each is asserted against the pinned image"
# The family's decision rule is `rules["A13"]` in
# benchmarks/cmd/bakeoff/rules.go. Both arms SHIP -- §7.2 asks for both and
# says "protocol choice explicit" -- so the "winner" is which protocol a caller
# gets by not choosing, not which one is deleted. The rule fixes it at amqp10
# because the row's own criterion is read on feature completeness on Artemis
# FIRST, where that arm covers nineteen of §4.8's thirty-nine features against
# STOMP's fifteen; and it records what STOMP cannot do, headed by the NACK
# finding, as the reason it is not the default.
